# What never belongs in a build context.
#
# There was no .dockerignore, so every `docker build` tarred the entire working tree and
# handed it to the daemon: a virtualenv, two node_modules trees and the git history, none of
# which any Dockerfile copies. On this repository that is well over half a gigabyte per
# build, before counting developer worktrees.
#
# It also cost cache hits. BuildKit fingerprints the context, so a file the image never
# contained — a local .env, a stray .pyc, a test run's .pytest_cache — still invalidated
# layers and forced `uv sync` to run again.
#
# Nothing here changes what ends up in an image. The Dockerfiles copy named paths
# (`pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock`, `libs/`, `services/<name>/`), so this only stops the daemon
# being sent files it was going to ignore anyway.

# Version control and agent scratch space.
.git
.gitignore
.claude

# Python environments and caches.
.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.mypy_cache
**/.pytest_cache
**/.ruff_cache

# JSON contracts, not a workspace package. Account's permission manifests reach it by bind mount
# (compose.prod.yml), so no image needs them — and leaving them in made a regenerated OpenAPI file
# a new layer under every image, which `remote.sh` then had to treat as "rebuild everything".
libs/contracts

# Node. No service image contains any frontend code.
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/dist
frontend

# Secrets and local configuration. A real .env must never reach the daemon, let alone a
# layer; `.env.example` is committed and carries no values.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# Local infrastructure and tooling output.
tools/dev-bootstrap/keys
*.log
.DS_Store

# Documentation and CI definitions — read by humans and GitHub, never by a runtime image.
docs
.github
e2e
